#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068446 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068839 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52f2cc01-7206-5396-aeef-8e4a73809186" :knowledge-point/title "Problems Involving Two Unknown Corresponding Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "142170e2-0b2d-5612-92d8-7c989aa1453e" :question/math-academy-id "e-10736" :question/problem "![](images/30/30081d2bd598ed395c5f9ac372b0d9899cc3e6f7ba0a26fefb27a2729b3dbef3.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$." :question/worked-solution "Since the angles $\\angle{AJF}$ and $\\angle{CIF}$ are corresponding angles, they are congruent and therefore have the same measure:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠AJF &= m∠CIF \\\\\n9x - 10^{∘} &= 7x + 4^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have an equation that we can solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n9x - 10^{∘} &= 7x + 4^{∘} \\\\\n9x - 7x &= 4^{∘} + 10^{∘} \\\\\n2x &= 14^{∘} \\\\\n\\frac{2x}{2} &= \\frac{14^{∘}}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{2x}{2} &= 7^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 7^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068450 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67fd827a-5a7a-5ab7-8153-a86e55ccd154" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems With Two Transversals"}] :question/id #uuid "40e3c6c8-c86a-55bf-80ef-d8845828ad27" :question/math-academy-id "e-10855" :question/problem "![](images/c1/c1d1410979b84b183e64f0537af77acde9cba2fabd14cf66eb90b33c4a388649.png)\n\nGiven that $r$, $s$ and $t$ in the above diagram are parallel, find $m\\angle{1}$." :question/worked-solution "Consider $\\angle 2$ and $\\angle 3$ in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/ca/cab8ca51722f9b2079db0f4c81fc0f89f26e23f99430ffac7ba709d945b6b08e.png)\n\nNote that $\\angle 2$ and the angle whose measure is $100^\\circ$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore,\n\n$$\nm\\angle 2 = 100^\\circ\n$$\n\nSimilarly, note that $\\angle 3$ and the angle whose measure is $50^\\circ$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore,\n\n$$\nm\\angle 3 = 50^\\circ\n$$\n\nLet's add these measures to our diagram:\n\n![](images/21/215845989e689c4a479101a1e4f6815e884f86c328b0991a02a18e434640aceb.png)\n\nThe angles $\\angle 1$, $\\angle 2$, and $\\angle 3$ combine to give a straight angle. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠1 + m∠2 + m∠3 &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠1 + 100^{∘} + 50^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠1 + 150^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nm∠1 &= 30^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068452 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85cc8613-7372-5e19-be2d-afd8d58d4708" :knowledge-point/title "Problems Involving an Unknown Corresponding Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "b983a05e-715b-5541-be19-cefac6f82b10" :question/math-academy-id "e-11054" :question/problem "![](images/c4/c49d6d86122df4b39745e6e917c38b50efbeb7b5209d1a24e3b8eb19459f2326.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB} \\parallel \\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{CD}$." :question/worked-solution "Since the angles $\\angle{CIF}$ and $\\angle{AJI}$ are corresponding angles, they are congruent and therefore have the same measure:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠CIF &= m∠AJI \\\\\n5x + 10^{∘} &= 110^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have an equation that we can solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5x + 10^{∘} &= 110^{∘} \\\\\n5x &= 100^{∘} \\\\\n\\frac{5x}{5} &= \\frac{100^{∘}}{5} \\\\\n\\frac{5x}{5} &= 20^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 20^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068453 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068846 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1cb30b2e-2f71-55a0-a80a-f7a0f5e71780" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Problems With Two Transversals That Require Solving an Equation"}] :question/id #uuid "c7d91325-bc8d-571d-8ae7-aa66ae0534c7" :question/math-academy-id "e-11055" :question/problem "![](images/60/606635acf3efd4f00a82cbc849d11bc6c67543357c84bb37344c9836a2c249e0.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $s \\parallel t$." :question/worked-solution "Consider $\\angle 1$ and $\\angle 2$ in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/23/23f64f62e4a78ccc60e1f2461dc9962f0eac00edac475305c9550419afe956bd.png)\n\nNote that $\\angle 1$ and the angle whose measure is $x$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore,\n\n$$\nm\\angle 1 = x\n$$\n\nSimilarly, note that $\\angle 2$ and the angle whose measure is $2x$ are corresponding angles formed by the same transversal. Therefore,\n\n$$\nm\\angle 2 = 2x\n$$\n\nLet's add these measures to our diagram.\n\n![](images/74/74448c1e414d472be9e80c8a78e5517f0c431e5e7dde5e21bf3972e137af529c.png)\n\nThe angles $\\angle 1$, $\\angle 2$, and the third angle whose measure is $45^\\circ$, combine to give a straight angle. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠1 + m∠2 + 45^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\nx + 2x + 45^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n3x + 45^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n3x &= 135^{∘} \\\\\n\\frac{3x}{3} &= \\frac{135^{∘}}{3} \\\\\n\\frac{3x}{3} &= 45^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 45^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069332 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85cc8613-7372-5e19-be2d-afd8d58d4708" :knowledge-point/title "Problems Involving an Unknown Corresponding Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81238a4a-5c7b-5e5d-afe6-08b8513f8272" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067163} {:answer/id #uuid "9fe3387e-16a3-5f7e-953b-881771149355" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067164} {:answer/id #uuid "b076e881-6df0-59e5-99a2-4c04194a5148" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067165} {:answer/id #uuid "f0a2cd29-f0d2-58a4-8646-b24308a67fa7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067166} {:answer/id #uuid "19b14bb4-ec84-54cc-984a-f74c7b155fa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067167}] :answer-field/id #uuid "ff03f103-fbbc-566d-8f3a-c5a0ed23f5dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186067162}] :question/id #uuid "f4e65be3-24c7-5463-8001-08ac073feca7" :question/math-academy-id "q-4170" :question/problem "![](images/c8/c89ac7325d47cb580e1479a90be32f923df0f17f8c478ad7265d135f4b1f4738.png)\n\nSolve for $x$ in the figure above given that $AB^(⟷)∥CD^(⟷)$."}]}
